    {
      "id": "fpsyyat",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Meaning of Matter"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Matter is anything that occupies space and possesses mass.",
        "Matter exists primarily in solid, liquid, and gaseous states.",
        "Changes in the states of matter occur due to alterations in temperature and pressure.",
        "Density is a fundamental physical property defined as mass per unit volume.",
        "Elasticity measures the tendency of a material to resume its original shape after deformation.",
        "Pressure measures the perpendicular force exerted per unit surface area."
      ],
      "id": "iun798k"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mktfsuy",
      "content": "• Matter is anything that has mass and occupies space."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5cu2gdq",
      "content": "• It is made up of tiny particles called atoms and molecules."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0mvvjxy",
      "content": "• Matter exists in different forms in nature."
    },
    {
      "id": "xwjanny",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. States of Matter"
    },
    {
      "id": "q4ya2qq",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>State</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Properties</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Examples</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Solid</p>",
            "<p>Fixed shape and volume</p>",
            "<p>Iron, wood, ice</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Liquid</p>",
            "<p>Fixed volume but no fixed shape</p>",
            "<p>Water, oil, milk</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Gas</p>",
            "<p>No fixed shape or volume</p>",
            "<p>Air, oxygen, carbon dioxide</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "qpyrxbv",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Physical Properties of Matter"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6p7alr8",
      "content": "• Mass – amount of matter in a substance."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2yfovtm",
      "content": "• Volume – space occupied by matter."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0rc0ejt",
      "content": "• Density – mass per unit volume."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1ahhdae",
      "content": "• Elasticity – ability of a material to regain its original shape."
    },
    {
      "id": "8c0y77b",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Changes in States of Matter"
    },
    {
      "id": "wao7vox",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Process</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Description</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Melting</p>",
            "<p>Solid changes into liquid</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Freezing</p>",
            "<p>Liquid changes into solid</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Evaporation</p>",
            "<p>Liquid changes into gas</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Condensation</p>",
            "<p>Gas changes into liquid</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Sublimation</p>",
            "<p>Solid changes directly into gas</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "yxvqiep",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Density"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wnatrgd",
      "content": "Density is defined as mass per unit volume."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Property",
        "Definition",
        "Key Formula / Unit"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Density",
          "Mass per unit volume of a substance",
          "Density = Mass / Volume (kg/m³)"
        ],
        [
          "Elasticity",
          "Ability of a body to regain original shape after deforming force is removed",
          "Modulus = Stress / Strain (N/m²)"
        ],
        [
          "Pressure",
          "Perpendicular force exerted per unit area of a surface",
          "Pressure = Force / Area (Pascal / Pa)"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "35qtrj4"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "i6350es",
      "content": "Formula: Density = Mass / Volume"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zbp972l",
      "content": "SI Unit of Density = kg/m³"
    },
    {
      "id": "hu55ucj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Elasticity"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ujzbcg5",
      "content": "Elasticity is the property of a material to regain its original shape after deformation when the external force is removed."
    },
    {
      "id": "bzoor1d",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. Pressure"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "un9kkub",
      "content": "Pressure is defined as force acting per unit area."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "r76zryr",
      "content": "Formula: Pressure = Force / Area"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "r3srq04",
      "content": "SI Unit = Pascal (Pa)"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Formulae and SI units for Density (kg/m³) and Pressure (Pascal or N/m²) are high-yield exam topics.",
        "Phase change phenomena (sublimation, vaporization, melting point variations) are frequently asked in conceptual MCQs.",
        "Understand stress-strain relationships and Hooke's Law under the topic of Elasticity."
      ],
      "id": "1r6jyo5"
    },
    {
      "id": "i2s1k9o",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Important Facts"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "p78dpuq",
      "content": "• Matter has mass and occupies space."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9dkbikk",
      "content": "• Density = Mass / Volume."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ltjxjs3",
      "content": "• SI unit of pressure is Pascal."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bugd9s2",
      "content": "• There are three main states of matter: solid, liquid and gas."
    }
  ],
  "enriched": true
